          Executive Summary: Multi-Policy Bundling Realities
        </div>
        <ul class="callout-list">
          <li><strong>Average National Savings:</strong> American consumers save an average of <strong>$782 per year (16.2% discount)</strong> when combining home and auto coverage with a single carrier.</li>
          <li><strong>The Single Deductible Advantage:</strong> Many bundled insurers offer a unified disaster deductible. If a severe hail storm damages both your home's roof and your car parked in the driveway, you pay only one deductible instead of two.</li>
          <li><strong>The "Shadow Mark-up" Warning:</strong> In 18% of evaluated households, purchasing policies separately from two distinct specialist insurers actually proved cheaper than an advertised bundle from a single national carrier.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="lead-text">
        Every major television commercial insists that bundling your home and auto insurance is the fastest way to save money. But while multi-policy discounts are undeniably real, the underlying math is frequently distorted by aggressive marketing.
      </p>

      <h2>Average Home & Auto Bundle Discounts by Major Insurer</h2>
