      <h2>Why Do Insurance Companies Give Such Huge Discounts for Bundling?</h2>
      <p>
        Carriers do not offer double-digit discounts out of altruism. The strategy is grounded in two harsh actuarial realities:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Customer Retention & Churn Reduction:</strong> An auto insurance customer has an average policyholder lifespan of 3.2 years before re-shopping. However, a customer with a bundled home and auto policy stays with the carrier for an average of <strong>8.4 years</strong>. Switching two policies simultaneously creates administrative friction that retains customers.</li>
        <li><strong>Superior Underwriting Risk Profiles:</strong> Statistically, homeowners are far more financially stable than the general driving population. Homeowners maintain lower loss ratios on their vehicles, making them highly profitable auto insurance policyholders.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>When Bundling Actually Costs You MORE Money</h2>
      <p>
        Before blindly accepting a bundle offer, beware of these three common financial scenarios:
      </p>

      <div class="card" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">The Three Unbundle Traps:</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>You Live in a Catastrophe Weather Zone:</strong> If your home is in coastal Florida, coastal Texas, or wildfire-adjacent California, standard carriers may quote $5,500 for homeowners insurance. A specialized regional property insurer might quote $3,600. Saving 15% on your $1,800 auto policy ($270) does not offset paying $1,900 too much for your home policy.</li>
          <li><strong>You Have a High-Risk Driver in the Household:</strong> If your household includes a driver with a recent DUI or multiple at-fault accidents, standard preferred bundle carriers will surcharge the auto policy massively. You are better off putting the high-risk driver on a standalone non-standard auto policy while keeping your home with a premier mutual carrier.</li>
          <li><strong>The "Affiliate Underwriter" Illusion:</strong> Some carriers advertise home/auto bundles, but when you look at the policy declarations, the home insurance is underwritten by an unaffiliated third-party agency (such as Homesite or American Modern). In these cases, claims handling is bifurcated and single-deductible perks do not apply.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>The Best Way to Shop for a Multi-Policy Package</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Obtain separate standalone quotes for your vehicle and your home from the best three independent carriers in your state.</li>
        <li>Request a bundled quote from each carrier.</li>
        <li>Calculate the blended household bottom line: (Standalone Auto + Standalone Home) vs. (Bundled Auto + Bundled Home).</li>
        <li>Review coverage limits carefully to ensure the bundled proposal does not secretly increase your deductibles or reduce personal liability coverage.</li>
      </ol>

